course: dictionary
lesson: 16072
title: Waif: a lost child or animal
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A waif is a child or animal with no home and nobody to look after it. Waifs are often thin, cold and hungry, and the word makes us feel sorry for them. «The waif curled up in the doorway, cold and hungry, with no home to go to.» The clues are cold, hungry and no home, so a waif is someone in need of help.
explain: The word can also describe a person who looks very thin, pale and delicate, as if they need looking after. This is an informal use, so people use it mostly in speech and stories. «After her long illness, she looked like a pale little waif.» The clues are pale and little, so this waif is a person who looks fragile, not one who lives on the streets.
explain: Check your meaning by swapping it into the sentence. Could you put «lost, hungry child» in place of waif and still make sense? If yes, you have the first meaning. Look for clues about home, food and feelings to see which meaning the writer uses.
fact: A waif is a homeless, neglected child or animal, often thin and with nobody to care for it.
fact: In Victorian stories, waifs were poor orphans living on the streets, like the children Dr Barnardo helped.
fact: The phrase «waifs and strays» means homeless children or lost animals.
fact: In The Wind in the Willows, Rat and Mole search for a lost baby otter, a little waif.
fact: Informally, «waif» can mean a very thin, pale, delicate-looking person. The clues in the sentence show which meaning is being used.
exercise: «The waif curled up in the doorway, cold and hungry, with no home to go to.» What is a waif here? | A tall tree in the park | *A homeless child or animal with nobody to care for it | A cheerful, rich shopkeeper | A large, comfortable house
exercise: «The waif of a kitten had been shivering under a bin for days.» What does waif suggest about the kitten? | It had just won a prize | It was a famous show cat | It was a rich and well-fed cat | *It had no home and nobody to care for it
exercise: Sam says «waif» means a rich, happy child. What is the right meaning? | A grown-up who likes to shop | A child who wins every race | *A homeless, neglected child or animal | A happy child on holiday by the sea
test: «After her long illness she looked like a pale waif, thin and delicate.» In this sentence, waif means | a homeless child in the street | a famous actor | *a very thin, pale person | a lost baby otter
test: «Rat and Mole searched the river bank for the lost baby otter, a little waif.» Why does the author call the otter a waif? | *Because it is a lost, homeless baby with nobody to care for it | A grown-up otter who swims fast | Because it is very large | Because it likes to play with Rat and Mole
exam: «The Victorian waifs slept in doorways.» Which feeling does the word waif usually bring to a reader? | Excitement about a race | Boredom | *Pity | Anger at a rude shopkeeper
